\wg21_head(
  title = {Accurate floating-point multiplication with a power of 10}
){
\dl{
  \dt{Document number:} \dd{\docnum{D4391R0}}
  \dt{Date:}            \dd{\tt{2026-10-14}}
  \dt{Audience:}        \dd{SG6}
  \dt{Project:}         \dd{ISO/IEC 14882 Programming Languages — C++, ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG21}
  \dt{Reply-to:}        \dd{Jan Schultke <\mail{janschultke@gmail.com}>\br
                            Matt Borland <\mail{matt@mattborland.com}>\br
                            Lénárd Szolnoki <\mail{cpp@lenardszolnoki.com}>}
  \dt{GitHub Issue:}    \dd{\ref("https://wg21.link/P4391/github")}
  \dt{Source:}          \dd{\ref("https://github.com/eisenwave/cpp-proposals/blob/main/src/mul-pow10.cow")}
}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
For the purpose of implementing \tcode{std::from_chars},
all standard libraries have a function
for accurately multiplying floating-point numbers by a power of 10.
We propose exposing that capability to the user.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

For the purpose of implementing \tcode{std::from_chars},
all standard libraries have a function
for accurately multiplying floating-point numbers by a power of 10.
This is innately necessary.

\style{
.borders td {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
}
.center {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}
}

\table(class="borders center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Standard library}
    \th{Functions}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{libstdc++}
    \td{
      \ref("https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/b24bee5c07afb8c90c02597527871a44b00c4fd5/libstdc%2B%2B-v3/src/c%2B%2B17/fast_float/fast_float.h#L2962"){\tt{from_chars_advanced}} →\br
      \ref("https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/b24bee5c07afb8c90c02597527871a44b00c4fd5/libstdc%2B%2B-v3/src/c%2B%2B17/fast_float/fast_float.h#L1548"){\tt{fast_float::compute_float}}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{libc++}
    \td{
      \ref("https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/libcxx/src/include/from_chars_floating_point.h#L320"){\tt{__from_chars_floating_point_decimal}} →\br
      \ref("https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/6fafdb34cd9038e52a270575e01f9bf16be89eac/libc/src/__support/str_to_float.h#L680"){\tt{decimal_exp_to_float}}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{MSVC STL}
    \td{
      \ref("https://github.com/microsoft/STL/blob/da52dc0fcadbd83690cfd1ed32408d2847439493/stl/inc/charconv#L1376"){\tt{_Convert_decimal_string_to_floating_type}} →\br
      \ref("https://github.com/microsoft/STL/blob/da52dc0fcadbd83690cfd1ed32408d2847439493/stl/inc/charconv#L1180"){\tt{_Assemble_floating_point_value}}
    }
  }
}

To understand how it is used, consider how \tcode{"123.5"}
gets converted to a floating-point number:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{"123.5"} is parsed as an integer to \tcode{1235},
    with a base-10 exponent of \tcode{-1}.
    The mathematical value is
    \math{\mrow{\mn{1235}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mn{-1}}}}.
    Decimal digits past a certain point are simply ignored;
    the integer is formed from the most significant decimal digits in the string.
  }
  \li{
    An internal function such as
    \tcode{std::float64_t mul_pow10(std::uint64_t significand, int exponent)}
    is called to calculate the result precisely,
    like \tcode{mul_pow10(1235, -1)}.
    The function doesn't need to be correctly rounded,
    but it needs to be precise enough to ensure round-tripping
    between \tcode{std::to_chars} and \tcode{std::from_chars},
    which requires proper results for at least 57 integer bits
    (but not correct rounding to 57 bits).
  }
}

\Bnote{
While multiplying with \tcode{std::pow(10, exponent)} ostensibly does the same,
it is significantly less precise and overflows or underflows in cases
where a \tcode{std::mul_pow10} function wouldn't
(like when combining a large significand with a hugely negative exponent).

In general,
lots of floating-point operations exist solely to fuse other operations,
like \tcode{std::hypot}, \tcode{std::fma}, etc.
}

\h2{Motivation}

\tcode{std::mul_pow10} is a necessary component
of parsing floating-point numbers with round-tripping,
is generally useful, and
is incredibly hard to implement.
\tcode{std::from_chars} can be seen as a higher-level wrapper,
and if the user wants to make their own variant of \tcode{std::from_chars}
which also supports digit separators and optional integer parts
(e.g. to parse floating-point numbers in their own language),
they have no means of \q{skipping} \tcode{std::from_chars}
and going straight to the low-level numeric utility it wraps.

The implementation also involves large lookup tables,
so even if the user implemented \tcode{std::mul_pow10} themselves,
they would end up pointlessly duplicating the lookup tables in the runtime library.
This is a problem that only the standard can solve, not the ecosystem.

\h2{Design}

\h3{API}

We propose the following set of functions in \header{numeric}:

\cppblock{
template<\exposid{cv-unqualified-float} F, \exposid{signed-or-unsigned} T>
  constexpr F mul_pow10(T n, int exp);
template<\exposid{cv-unqualified-float} F, \exposid{signed-or-unsigned} T>
  constexpr F cr_mul_pow10(T n, int exp);
}

\tcode{mul_pow10} computes
\math{\mrow{\mi{n}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{exp}}}} without correct rounding
but with sufficient rounding for \tcode{std::from_chars} to round-trip for type \tcode{F}.

\tcode{mul_pow10} computes the same with correct rounding,
but it is implementation-defined which specializations are allowed (possibly none).
Requiring correct rounding for all type combinations requires unreasonable implementation effort,
but if the implementation provides a correctly rounded function anyway,
it would be frustrating not to have any access to it.
While such a function is innately not portable,
the user can detect support using \tcode{requires} expressions.

\h3{Handling big integers}

Since the API is constrained to accept
any signed or unsigned integer type \tcode{T},
this raises the question how huge integers
such as \tcode{__int128} or \tcode{_BitInt(1024)} are handled.
Correct rounding for \tcode{mul_pow10} would impose an
unreasonable implementation burden since in the worst case,
every integer bit can affect the result;
we cannot simply truncate the less significant bits.

However, \tcode{mul_pow10}
only needs to be sufficiently precise to ensure round-tripping,
meaning that only a subset of integer inputs have a decent approximation.

\Bex{
For 128-bit integers and \tcode{F = std::float64_t},
57 integer bits are sufficient for round-tripping.
We can implement this specialization as follows:
\cppblock{
using __u128 = unsigned __int128;
using __i128 = signed __int128;

float64_t mul_pow10(__u128 n, int exp) {
  // 1. Select the uppermost 64 nonzero bits in the input.
  int trunc_bits = max(64 - countl_zero(n), 0);
  auto m = uint64_t(n >> trunc_bits);

  // 2. Use the underlying mul_pow10 for uint64_t and float64_t,
  //    with the same decimal exponent.
  //    In practice, this function is correctly rounded,
  //    i.e. cr_mul_pow10(m, exp) is available.
  float64_t trunc_result = mul_pow10(m, exp);

  // 3. Shift to the left
  //    by the same amount that n has been shifted to the right.
  //    Since float64_t is a binary float,
  //    this operation merely adjusts the binary exponent
  //    and introduces no further imprecision.
  return ldexp(trunc_result, trunc_bits);
}

float64_t mul_pow10(__i128 n, int exp) {
  // For any signed integer,
  // we can delegate to the corresponding unsigned implementation.
  if (n < 0) {
    // Unsigned negation guards against signed integer overflow.
    // copysign ensures that we produce negative zero on underflow.
    return copysign(mul_pow10(-__u128(n), exp), -1f64);
  }
  return mul_pow10(__u128(n), exp);
}
}
}

This example is not specific to 128-bit integers
and can be easily generalized to any width.
The key insight is that while all integer bits would be necessary for correct rounding,
only some of the leading bits are necessary for round-tripping,
so any less significant bits can be truncated, for any integer width.

\h3{Why not use a floating-point operand?}

One could also imagine a \header{cmath} operation like
\cppblock{
std::float64_t scal10n(std::float64_t x, int exp);
}

However, this would not be sufficiently precise to implement \tcode{std::from_chars}.
For binary64 format,
at least 57 integer bits of precision are required for round-tripping,
but \tcode{std::float64_t} can only represent 53 bits.

This is also why we don't propose a \header{cmath} operation that is C-compatible.

\h3{Decimal floating-point}

Decimal floating-point numbers are a special case,
and are somewhat supported by C++;
\tcode{numeric_limits<F>::radix} may be \tcode{10},
although there is no standard C++ spelling for decimal floating-point types,
unlike C, which offers \tcode{_Decimal64} et al.

The decimal case is usually trivial because it is always correctly rounded
and can be implemented in terms of
\tcode{std::scalbn(_Decimal64(n), exp)}.
Also, under the hood,
ISO/IEC 60559 decimal floating-point types are an integer and decimal exponent,
and any standard library providing support for them needs functions
for converting between integers-and-exponent pairs and floating-point numbers.
\tcode{std::mul_pow10} is simply exposing those otherwise internal functions.

\Bnote{
There is one edge case for decimal floating-point:
huge numbers like those representable by \tcode{_BitInt(1'000'000)}
may result in infinity when converted to \tcode{_Decimal32},
despite the mathematical result of, say,
\tcode{std::scalbn(_Decimal32(n), -10'000)} being finite.

A robust approach that handles this case instead
correctly rounds the input \tcode{_BitInt(1'000'000)} to
however many integer bits fit into \tcode{_Decimal32}
and sets the exponent field.
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

Add a feature-test macro to \eelis{version.syn} as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_mul_pow10 20XXXXL // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<header>}}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[numeric.ops.overview]}

Change the synopsis in \eelis{numeric.ops.overview} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
//\serif{ mostly freestanding}
namespace std {
  \etc
  template<class T, class U>
    constexpr T saturating_cast(U x) noexcept;

\ins{  //\serif{ [numeric.mul.pow10], multiplication with a power of 10}
  template<class F, class T>
    constexpr F mul_pow10(T n, int exp) noexcept;       //\serif{ freestanding-deleted}
  template<class F, class T>
    constexpr F cr_mul_pow10(T n, int exp) noexcept;    //\serif{ freestanding-deleted}}
}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[numeric.mul.pow10]}

At the end of \eelis{numeric.ops},
append a new subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Multiplication with a power of 10 \stable_ref{numeric.mul.pow10}}
\cowel_var_set("pnum", 0)

\pnum
In either of the following function descriptions,
if the result is exactly representable in type \tcode{F},
no floating-point exceptions are raised;
otherwise, the raised floating-point exceptions are a property of the implementation.
Furthermore, if \tcode{numeric_limits<F>::radix == 10} is \tcode{true},
the provided exponent \tcode{exp} is the preferred quantum exponent of the result.

\itemdecl{
template<class F, class T>
  constexpr F mul_pow10(T n, int exp) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{F} is a cv-unqualified floating-point type\iref{basic.fundamental}.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type.
  }
}

\pnum
\constantwhen
The rounded result is finite and
is zero if and only if \tcode{n} is zero.

\pnum
\effects
If \tcode{cr_mul_pow10<F>(n, exp)} is well-formed,
equivalent to:
\tcode{return cr_mul_pow10<F>(n, exp);}
Otherwise, returns
\math{\mrow{\mi{n}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{exp}}}}.

\pnum
\remarks
If the result is not correctly rounded,
let \tcode{U} be a hypothetical integer type
so that any finite value \math{\mi{x}} of type \tcode{F}
can be decomposed into
\ul{
  \li{
    a value \tcode{m} of type \tcode{U} and
  }
  \li{
    a corresponding base-10 exponent \tcode{e} of type \tcode{int}
  }
}
so that \math{\mi{x}} equals \tcode{mul_pow10<F>(m, e)}.
The result of this function is at least as accurate as \tcode{mul_pow10<F>(U(m), e)} and
depends solely on the unrounded result.
The result has negative sign bit if \tcode{n} is negative.

\example{
\tcode{mul_pow10<F>(42, 1)} equals \tcode{mul_pow10<F>(420, 0)}
because the unrounded result is \math{\mn{420}}.
If \tcode{F} cannot represent 420,
both these expressions are rounded to the same result.
\tcode{mul_pow10<F>(-1, -1'000'000)}
is negative zero if \tcode{F} has insufficient precision
to represent the unrounded result.
}
}
}

\editnote{
The invention of a hypothetical integer type is necessary
because we need to work with a type where the round-tripping requirement is actually feasible.
Obviously, we cannot decompose a \tcode{std::float128_t}
into a \tcode{std::uint8_t} and base-10 exponent;
we need a fairly wide integer type for the significand.

However, we can require that the specialization
\tcode{mul_pow10<std::float128_t, std::uint8_t>}
is as precise as
\tcode{mul_pow10<std::float128_t, unsigned __int128>}
(possibly implemented by just calling that more precise function).
}

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
template<class F, class T>
  constexpr F cr_mul_pow10(T n, int exp) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{F} is a cv-unqualified floating-point type\iref{basic.fundamental}.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type.
  }
  \li{
    Either \tcode{numeric_limits<F>::radix == 10} is \tcode{true} or
    the pair of types \tcode{F} and \tcode{T}
    belongs to an implementation-defined set
    for which this operation is supported.
    If the pair \tcode{F} and \tcode{T} belongs to this set and
    \tcode{T} is unsigned,
    then all pairs \tcode{F} and \tcode{U} belong to this set,
    where \tcode{U} is any integer type
    whose width is less than or equal to that of \tcode{T}.
  }
}

\pnum
\constantwhen
The rounded result is finite and
is zero if and only if \tcode{n} is zero.


\pnum
\returns
\math{\mrow{\mi{n}\mo{×}\msup{\mn{10}\mi{exp}}}}
correctly rounded.
}
}

